Offering this vintage 1970 dune-buggy rear sub-frame as shown. This is a Hadley TransVair setup for cutting the frame horns off a VW floor pan and installing this cage, using a Corvair engine and an early-model ('60-'64) Corvair transaxle.  This is a a professional jig-&-mig-welded unit; the previously-removed ID plate on the top of the rear frame tube is shown in Photo 4.  This Item was professionally engineered and constructed by Hadley Chassis Engineering in Costa Mesa, California.
Although not part of the original TransVair sub-frame, the previous owner added a pair of bumper guards to the rear, as shown in the pictures.
Dimensions are:
51" OAL, not counting add-on bumper guards;
39" wide at front brackets;
Width of 42-1/2" at shock brackets;
Main cradle -- 14" high;
Bottom of cradle to top of shock brackets -- 19-1/2" high;
Radius at rear corners approximately 7";
Main frame tubes -- 1-1/4"; support tubes -- 1";
Lower slotted frame-mount pads -- 5-1/2" tall x 2" wide x 1/4" thick at approximate 30° rearward angle.
The first four pictures show it right-side-up; the next five show it upside-down. Photo 1 shows it from the driver's (left) side; Photo 2 shows the right side.
Photos 10-14: The transaxle crossmember adapter-mount assembly that fits the early-model (1960-'64) Corvairs is also shown mounted to a '64 Corvair transaxle (Photos 10-12), then shown as just the assembly (Photos 13,14).  The mount kit is also included -- the transaxle is not.
Also included is the 1971 magazine The Complete Volkswagen Book No. 2 by Petersen Publishing Company that shows the kit and assembly instructions from pages 132 to 143.  Last two pictures.

